{
  "version": "2.1.0",
  "$schema": "https://json.schemastore.org/sarif-2.1.0.json",
  "runs": [
    {
      "tool": {
        "driver": {
          "name": "Strix",
          "informationUri": "https://strix.ai",
          "rules": [
            {
              "id": "CWE-345",
              "name": "Planted run file in the runs folder makes cra status report a healthy latest run",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Planted run file in the runs folder makes cra status report a healthy latest run"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A run record placed in the `--runs` folder with a newer `finished_at` and self-declared healthy fields replaces the genuine latest run in `cra status`. The status output then reads \"Latest run: all sources ok.\" and exits 0, hiding a genuine REPORT CASE finding that the real run recorded."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A run record placed in the `--runs` folder with a newer `finished_at` and self-declared healthy fields replaces the genuine latest run in `cra status`. The status output then reads \"Latest run: all sources ok.\" and exits 0, hiding a genuine REPORT CASE finding that the real run recorded.\n\nThe `status` command is the operator's only safety signal between scheduled `cra run` calls. With write access to the runs folder, a single file hides an earlier REPORT CASE warning and makes status exit 0 with a fresh \"last successful run\" time. The integrity of the status verdict depends entirely on the folder contents. Confidentiality and availability are not affected. Integrity impact is limited to the operator-visible status verdict.\n\nTreat the status verdict as only as trustworthy as the runs folder, and make that explicit. Two options for the project owner to decide. (a) Add an integrity seal to each run record at save time (a digest of its canonical content, verified in `load_runs`, with unsealed or mismatched files treated as unreadable and surfaced as a warning). This stops accidental and naive edits, not a determined local writer, and needs a decision on key handling. (b) Keep the current design but have `status` print a visible warning when the newest run file is not the newest file written by the tool, and document the limit in the README. Whichever option is chosen, make sure a file that cannot be verified never produces \"Latest run: all sources ok.\".",
                "markdown": "A run record placed in the `--runs` folder with a newer `finished_at` and self-declared healthy fields replaces the genuine latest run in `cra status`. The status output then reads \"Latest run: all sources ok.\" and exits 0, hiding a genuine REPORT CASE finding that the real run recorded.\n\nThe `status` command is the operator's only safety signal between scheduled `cra run` calls. With write access to the runs folder, a single file hides an earlier REPORT CASE warning and makes status exit 0 with a fresh \"last successful run\" time. The integrity of the status verdict depends entirely on the folder contents. Confidentiality and availability are not affected. Integrity impact is limited to the operator-visible status verdict.\n\nTreat the status verdict as only as trustworthy as the runs folder, and make that explicit. Two options for the project owner to decide. (a) Add an integrity seal to each run record at save time (a digest of its canonical content, verified in `load_runs`, with unsealed or mismatched files treated as unreadable and surfaced as a warning). This stops accidental and naive edits, not a determined local writer, and needs a decision on key handling. (b) Keep the current design but have `status` print a visible warning when the newest run file is not the newest file written by the tool, and document the limit in the README. Whichever option is chosen, make sure a file that cannot be verified never produces \"Latest run: all sources ok.\"."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "3.3",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-345",
                  "stride:S",
                  "stride:T"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/345.html"
            },
            {
              "id": "CWE-770",
              "name": "cra status loads every planted run file into memory without a total size budget",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "cra status loads every planted run file into memory without a total size budget"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "`cra status` reads up to 1000 `*.json` files from the runs folder, each up to 20 MB, and keeps every parsed record in memory, although only the newest and the newest healthy record are used. A folder holding many large files can exhaust memory and make `status` fail."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "`cra status` reads up to 1000 `*.json` files from the runs folder, each up to 20 MB, and keeps every parsed record in memory, although only the newest and the newest healthy record are used. A folder holding many large files can exhaust memory and make `status` fail.\n\nDenial of service of the `status` command. A local writer to the runs folder can make `status` consume several gigabytes of memory, slow it down, or crash it with a MemoryError. The operator then cannot check the last run. Data is not disclosed and not modified.\n\nBound what `status` loads, not only the size of each file. Keep only the newest run and the newest run that passes the completeness check while reading, instead of retaining every record. Add a total byte budget for the run folder. Make a skipped or oversized file visible: if the budget or the count cap drops a newer file, `status` must print a warning and must not report \"Latest run: all sources ok.\", so that a dropped run cannot hide behind an older healthy run. The project owner should decide the budget size and the wording of that warning.",
                "markdown": "`cra status` reads up to 1000 `*.json` files from the runs folder, each up to 20 MB, and keeps every parsed record in memory, although only the newest and the newest healthy record are used. A folder holding many large files can exhaust memory and make `status` fail.\n\nDenial of service of the `status` command. A local writer to the runs folder can make `status` consume several gigabytes of memory, slow it down, or crash it with a MemoryError. The operator then cannot check the last run. Data is not disclosed and not modified.\n\nBound what `status` loads, not only the size of each file. Keep only the newest run and the newest run that passes the completeness check while reading, instead of retaining every record. Add a total byte budget for the run folder. Make a skipped or oversized file visible: if the budget or the count cap drops a newer file, `status` must print a warning and must not report \"Latest run: all sources ok.\", so that a dropped run cannot hide behind an older healthy run. The project owner should decide the budget size and the wording of that warning."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "3.3",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-770",
                  "stride:D"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/770.html"
            },
            {
              "id": "CWE-1289",
              "name": "Slash-separated or upper-case Maven purl is accepted as OK and silently clears an exploited component",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Slash-separated or upper-case Maven purl is accepted as OK and silently clears an exploited component"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A CycloneDX component whose Maven purl uses a slash-separated namespace, such as `pkg:maven/org/apache/logging/log4j/log4j-core@2.14.1`, or an upper-case group or artifact spelling, is accepted as a valid component. OSV does not match these spellings, so `cra run` reports the component as checked with zero findings, a complete result, and exit code 0. The canonical form of the same component is affected by CVE-2021-44228, which is listed in CISA KEV."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A CycloneDX component whose Maven purl uses a slash-separated namespace, such as `pkg:maven/org/apache/logging/log4j/log4j-core@2.14.1`, or an upper-case group or artifact spelling, is accepted as a valid component. OSV does not match these spellings, so `cra run` reports the component as checked with zero findings, a complete result, and exit code 0. The canonical form of the same component is affected by CVE-2021-44228, which is listed in CISA KEV.\n\nA supplier-supplied or hand-edited SBOM can make the tool report a vulnerable Maven component as clean. The run record is marked complete, the console prints `No exploited vulnerabilities found in the checked components (all sources ok)`, and `cra status` reports `Latest run: all sources ok.` A KEV-listed affected component is therefore never raised for the 24-hour early-warning decision. The same silence applies to every advisory for that component, not only the exploited one.\n\nReject Maven purl namespaces that contain a slash as non-canonical, since they must use dot-separated group ids. Refuse upper-case Maven group or artifact spellings as UNKNOWN until the tool can confirm the spelling OSV uses, because OSV's Maven matching is case-sensitive. Update the test that currently expects the slash form to be OK so that it expects UNKNOWN, and add regression tests for both forms. This converts the silent clean result for these spellings into UNKNOWN and RESULT INCOMPLETE for human review.",
                "markdown": "A CycloneDX component whose Maven purl uses a slash-separated namespace, such as `pkg:maven/org/apache/logging/log4j/log4j-core@2.14.1`, or an upper-case group or artifact spelling, is accepted as a valid component. OSV does not match these spellings, so `cra run` reports the component as checked with zero findings, a complete result, and exit code 0. The canonical form of the same component is affected by CVE-2021-44228, which is listed in CISA KEV.\n\nA supplier-supplied or hand-edited SBOM can make the tool report a vulnerable Maven component as clean. The run record is marked complete, the console prints `No exploited vulnerabilities found in the checked components (all sources ok)`, and `cra status` reports `Latest run: all sources ok.` A KEV-listed affected component is therefore never raised for the 24-hour early-warning decision. The same silence applies to every advisory for that component, not only the exploited one.\n\nReject Maven purl namespaces that contain a slash as non-canonical, since they must use dot-separated group ids. Refuse upper-case Maven group or artifact spellings as UNKNOWN until the tool can confirm the spelling OSV uses, because OSV's Maven matching is case-sensitive. Update the test that currently expects the slash form to be OK so that it expects UNKNOWN, and add regression tests for both forms. This converts the silent clean result for these spellings into UNKNOWN and RESULT INCOMPLETE for human review."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "5.5",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-1289",
                  "stride:T",
                  "stride:I"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/1289.html"
            },
            {
              "id": "CWE-697",
              "name": "Same-version range boundaries misordered, hiding reintroduced affected versions",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Same-version range boundaries misordered, hiding reintroduced affected versions"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The OSV range walk in the CRA matcher sorts a fixed boundary before a later introduced boundary at the same version. A version that is affected again after the reintroduction is therefore computed as not affected. The evaluation step then reports it as UNKNOWN, so an actively exploited (KEV-listed) affected component gets urgent human review instead of a report case."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The OSV range walk in the CRA matcher sorts a fixed boundary before a later introduced boundary at the same version. A version that is affected again after the reintroduction is therefore computed as not affected. The evaluation step then reports it as UNKNOWN, so an actively exploited (KEV-listed) affected component gets urgent human review instead of a report case.\n\nA component affected by a KEV-listed vulnerability through a same-version reintroduction is routed to URGENT_HUMAN_REVIEW instead of REPORT_CASE. The matcher's direct result for the same version is NOT_AFFECTED. Demonstrated with a crafted OSV-style record; the scope is limited to range shapes with a same-version end and start inside one range.\n\nKeep the order in which range events were written when two events share a version, instead of forcing every start before every end. Then the reintroduction at the same version is applied after the preceding fixed event, and the open span is kept. Add a regression test with a same-version fixed and introduced pair, asserting AFFECTED for a version after the reintroduction and an UNKNOWN or AFFECTED result (never NOT_AFFECTED) for a reversed-order tie.",
                "markdown": "The OSV range walk in the CRA matcher sorts a fixed boundary before a later introduced boundary at the same version. A version that is affected again after the reintroduction is therefore computed as not affected. The evaluation step then reports it as UNKNOWN, so an actively exploited (KEV-listed) affected component gets urgent human review instead of a report case.\n\nA component affected by a KEV-listed vulnerability through a same-version reintroduction is routed to URGENT_HUMAN_REVIEW instead of REPORT_CASE. The matcher's direct result for the same version is NOT_AFFECTED. Demonstrated with a crafted OSV-style record; the scope is limited to range shapes with a same-version end and start inside one range.\n\nKeep the order in which range events were written when two events share a version, instead of forcing every start before every end. Then the reintroduction at the same version is applied after the preceding fixed event, and the open span is kept. Add a regression test with a same-version fixed and introduced pair, asserting AFFECTED for a version after the reintroduction and an UNKNOWN or AFFECTED result (never NOT_AFFECTED) for a reversed-order tie."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "3.7",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-697",
                  "stride:T",
                  "stride:I"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/697.html"
            },
            {
              "id": "CWE-754",
              "name": "Unchecked components recorded as known not exploited when OSV gives no answer",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Unchecked components recorded as known not exploited when OSV gives no answer"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "When OSV gives no answer for a component, or the details of a listed advisory cannot be loaded or read, the matcher records the finding as exploited_known = True whenever the KEV list is fresh. The tool never compared that component's CVEs with KEV, yet the finding states that its exploitation status is known and routes it to ordinary human review rather than urgent review."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "When OSV gives no answer for a component, or the details of a listed advisory cannot be loaded or read, the matcher records the finding as exploited_known = True whenever the KEV list is fresh. The tool never compared that component's CVEs with KEV, yet the finding states that its exploitation status is known and routes it to ordinary human review rather than urgent review.\n\nAn affected component with an unanswered OSV query and a KEV-listed CVE is shown with exploited False and exploited_known True, and its action is HUMAN_REVIEW instead of URGENT_HUMAN_REVIEW. The output asserts a check that did not happen. The run is still marked RESULT INCOMPLETE, so the operator is warned, but the per-finding urgency and the stored finding record are wrong.\n\nOn the three paths where no CVE list was obtained (no OSV answer, advisory details missing, and an advisory that cannot be evaluated), record exploited_known as False, so the finding follows the same urgent-review rule that already applies to a failed KEV download. Apply the same invariant to components without a purl, which currently pass the KEV freshness as well; that change alters the urgency of every component without a purl, so the maintainers should confirm it as product policy before it is made.",
                "markdown": "When OSV gives no answer for a component, or the details of a listed advisory cannot be loaded or read, the matcher records the finding as exploited_known = True whenever the KEV list is fresh. The tool never compared that component's CVEs with KEV, yet the finding states that its exploitation status is known and routes it to ordinary human review rather than urgent review.\n\nAn affected component with an unanswered OSV query and a KEV-listed CVE is shown with exploited False and exploited_known True, and its action is HUMAN_REVIEW instead of URGENT_HUMAN_REVIEW. The output asserts a check that did not happen. The run is still marked RESULT INCOMPLETE, so the operator is warned, but the per-finding urgency and the stored finding record are wrong.\n\nOn the three paths where no CVE list was obtained (no OSV answer, advisory details missing, and an advisory that cannot be evaluated), record exploited_known as False, so the finding follows the same urgent-review rule that already applies to a failed KEV download. Apply the same invariant to components without a purl, which currently pass the KEV freshness as well; that change alters the urgency of every component without a purl, so the maintainers should confirm it as product policy before it is made."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "3.7",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-754",
                  "stride:T",
                  "stride:I"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/754.html"
            },
            {
              "id": "CWE-400",
              "name": "Total download deadline is not enforced during reads, so a slow response can stall a run indefinitely",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Total download deadline is not enforced during reads, so a slow response can stall a run indefinitely"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "`http_fetch` applies a 120-second total deadline, but checks it only between `read()` calls. `HTTPResponse.read(64 * 1024)` blocks until 64 KB arrive or the connection closes, so a server that sends a byte every few seconds keeps one read open and defeats the deadline. The per-read socket timeout only catches silence longer than 30 seconds, so a drip below that interval stalls `cra run` for as long as the server keeps dribbling bytes."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "`http_fetch` applies a 120-second total deadline, but checks it only between `read()` calls. `HTTPResponse.read(64 * 1024)` blocks until 64 KB arrive or the connection closes, so a server that sends a byte every few seconds keeps one read open and defeats the deadline. The per-read socket timeout only catches silence longer than 30 seconds, so a drip below that interval stalls `cra run` for as long as the server keeps dribbling bytes.\n\nAn on-path attacker who can serve OSV or KEV responses can make a single download take an unbounded time, which stops the scheduled `cra run` from finishing. No run record is written for the stalled run, so the last saved record ages until `cra status` reports the run as overdue. The check is interrupted only when the operator kills the process.\n\nRead the response in small pieces (for example with `read1`) so the deadline check runs after each network chunk, and keep the per-read socket timeout as the bound on silence. Add a regression test that uses a real drip-feeding stream rather than a mocked clock.",
                "markdown": "`http_fetch` applies a 120-second total deadline, but checks it only between `read()` calls. `HTTPResponse.read(64 * 1024)` blocks until 64 KB arrive or the connection closes, so a server that sends a byte every few seconds keeps one read open and defeats the deadline. The per-read socket timeout only catches silence longer than 30 seconds, so a drip below that interval stalls `cra run` for as long as the server keeps dribbling bytes.\n\nAn on-path attacker who can serve OSV or KEV responses can make a single download take an unbounded time, which stops the scheduled `cra run` from finishing. No run record is written for the stalled run, so the last saved record ages until `cra status` reports the run as overdue. The check is interrupted only when the operator kills the process.\n\nRead the response in small pieces (for example with `read1`) so the deadline check runs after each network chunk, and keep the per-read socket timeout as the bound on silence. Add a regression test that uses a real drip-feeding stream rather than a mocked clock."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "3.7",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-400",
                  "stride:D"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/400.html"
            },
            {
              "id": "CWE-755",
              "name": "OSV vulnerability id with a trailing dot aborts the whole run",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "OSV vulnerability id with a trailing dot aborts the whole run"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The id pattern used for OSV responses accepts an identifier that ends with a dot, but the file-name check used when the id is turned into a cache path rejects it. A feed response that lists such an id raises an uncaught `ValueError` inside `fetch_osv`, which aborts `cra run` for every component. The run is recorded as an internal error and is not completed."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The id pattern used for OSV responses accepts an identifier that ends with a dot, but the file-name check used when the id is turned into a cache path rejects it. A feed response that lists such an id raises an uncaught `ValueError` inside `fetch_osv`, which aborts `cra run` for every component. The run is recorded as an internal error and is not completed.\n\nAn on-path attacker who can serve one OSV batch response can stop every `cra run` from completing while that response is served. The tool fails safe (the run is marked incomplete and the operator is told), so no false \"nothing found\" result is produced; the impact is an availability loss for the scheduled check.\n\nMake the identifier pattern match exactly what the cache-path check accepts, so that a trailing dot is rejected when the batch answer is parsed. Also catch a rejected identifier at the call site in `fetch_osv` so that a single bad identifier cannot abort the whole run.",
                "markdown": "The id pattern used for OSV responses accepts an identifier that ends with a dot, but the file-name check used when the id is turned into a cache path rejects it. A feed response that lists such an id raises an uncaught `ValueError` inside `fetch_osv`, which aborts `cra run` for every component. The run is recorded as an internal error and is not completed.\n\nAn on-path attacker who can serve one OSV batch response can stop every `cra run` from completing while that response is served. The tool fails safe (the run is marked incomplete and the operator is told), so no false \"nothing found\" result is produced; the impact is an availability loss for the scheduled check.\n\nMake the identifier pattern match exactly what the cache-path check accepts, so that a trailing dot is rejected when the batch answer is parsed. Also catch a rejected identifier at the call site in `fetch_osv` so that a single bad identifier cannot abort the whole run."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "3.7",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-755",
                  "stride:T",
                  "stride:I"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/755.html"
            },
            {
              "id": "CWE-367",
              "name": "Concurrent run records in the same second overwrite each other",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Concurrent run records in the same second overwrite each other"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "`save_run` checks whether the run file name is free and then writes it in a separate step. Two runs that finish in the same UTC second can both see the name as free and both write it, so the later write replaces the earlier run record. In the measured case, 16 concurrent saves left one or two files on disk. A complete run can therefore replace an incomplete one in the same second, and `cra status` then shows the replacement."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "`save_run` checks whether the run file name is free and then writes it in a separate step. Two runs that finish in the same UTC second can both see the name as free and both write it, so the later write replaces the earlier run record. In the measured case, 16 concurrent saves left one or two files on disk. A complete run can therefore replace an incomplete one in the same second, and `cra status` then shows the replacement.\n\nTwo `cra run` processes that finish in the same second (for example an overlapping scheduled job and a manual run) can lose one run record. If the surviving record is a complete run that replaced an incomplete one, `cra status` reports a complete latest run and hides the failure. The impact is limited to the operator's own run history; no remote party can trigger it.\n\nClaim the run file name atomically before writing it, so that a second process cannot take the same name, and keep the atomic replace for the content. If the claim fails, move to the next numbered name. Add a regression test that runs concurrent saves in one second rather than only sequential ones.",
                "markdown": "`save_run` checks whether the run file name is free and then writes it in a separate step. Two runs that finish in the same UTC second can both see the name as free and both write it, so the later write replaces the earlier run record. In the measured case, 16 concurrent saves left one or two files on disk. A complete run can therefore replace an incomplete one in the same second, and `cra status` then shows the replacement.\n\nTwo `cra run` processes that finish in the same second (for example an overlapping scheduled job and a manual run) can lose one run record. If the surviving record is a complete run that replaced an incomplete one, `cra status` reports a complete latest run and hides the failure. The impact is limited to the operator's own run history; no remote party can trigger it.\n\nClaim the run file name atomically before writing it, so that a second process cannot take the same name, and keep the atomic replace for the content. If the claim fails, move to the next numbered name. Add a regression test that runs concurrent saves in one second rather than only sequential ones."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "2.5",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-367",
                  "stride:T",
                  "stride:I"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/367.html"
            },
            {
              "id": "CWE-390",
              "name": "Run record above 20 MB is silently skipped by cra status, which then reports the previous clean run as current",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Run record above 20 MB is silently skipped by cra status, which then reports the previous clean run as current"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "cra run saves its full result, including every finding, with no size limit, but cra status silently skips any run file over its 20 MB read cap. An SBOM with many duplicate components can push the saved run past that cap, so cra status reports an older complete run as the latest run with exit code 0, even when the skipped run contains a REPORT CASE."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "cra run saves its full result, including every finding, with no size limit, but cra status silently skips any run file over its 20 MB read cap. An SBOM with many duplicate components can push the saved run past that cap, so cra status reports an older complete run as the latest run with exit code 0, even when the skipped run contains a REPORT CASE.\n\nThe status command, which is the scheduled health check, can show a false all-clear while the latest run found an affected and actively exploited component. The run command itself still prints the REPORT CASE and exits with code 3, so the loss is limited to the status view and to operators who rely on it. The effect is an incorrect integrity signal for the early-warning check, with no confidentiality or availability impact.\n\nNever skip a run file silently. When a run file exists but is too large or unreadable, status must treat it as the latest run and report it as incomplete, using the file modification time as its time. Also keep saved runs within a size the reader accepts, or raise the reader limit to match the writer, so a legitimate run is not dropped.",
                "markdown": "cra run saves its full result, including every finding, with no size limit, but cra status silently skips any run file over its 20 MB read cap. An SBOM with many duplicate components can push the saved run past that cap, so cra status reports an older complete run as the latest run with exit code 0, even when the skipped run contains a REPORT CASE.\n\nThe status command, which is the scheduled health check, can show a false all-clear while the latest run found an affected and actively exploited component. The run command itself still prints the REPORT CASE and exits with code 3, so the loss is limited to the status view and to operators who rely on it. The effect is an incorrect integrity signal for the early-warning check, with no confidentiality or availability impact.\n\nNever skip a run file silently. When a run file exists but is too large or unreadable, status must treat it as the latest run and report it as incomplete, using the file modification time as its time. Also keep saved runs within a size the reader accepts, or raise the reader limit to match the writer, so a legitimate run is not dropped."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "3.1",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-390",
                  "stride:T",
                  "stride:I"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/390.html"
            },
            {
              "id": "strix-coverage/sast-static-triage-pass",
