      "results": [
        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-345",
          "ruleIndex": 0,
          "level": "note",
          "message": {
            "text": "Planted run file in the runs folder makes cra status report a healthy latest run\n\nA run record placed in the `--runs` folder with a newer `finished_at` and self-declared healthy fields replaces the genuine latest run in `cra status`. The status output then reads \"Latest run: all sources ok.\" and exits 0, hiding a genuine REPORT CASE finding that the real run recorded."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "src/news_watcher.py"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 585,
                  "endLine": 594
                }
              },
              "message": {
                "text": "Root control: run_is_complete trusts self-declared complete and sources flags with no cross-check or integrity check"
              }
            },
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "src/news_watcher.py"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 601,
                  "endLine": 612
                }
              },
              "message": {
                "text": "Loader: load_runs trusts every *.json file in the runs folder and sorts by its own finished_at"
              }
            },
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "src/news_watcher.py"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 658,
                  "endLine": 659
                }
              },
              "message": {
                "text": "Sink: status prints the healthy verdict from the file's own flags"
              }
            },
            {
              "logicalLocations": [
                {
                  "fullyQualifiedName": "cra status --runs &lt;folder&gt;",
                  "kind": "endpoint"
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
          "partialFingerprints": {
            "primaryLocationLineHash": "3d8c0537fed5b5c18da03cf9a0857db5d6d2a2742aa8542175039664ed0f07b4"
          },
          "properties": {
            "security-severity": "3.3",
            "strix_vuln_class_hash": "81c9d1e96545b147d25a29a15acc5a3853deffd6ed497b4cbe18a4ac862cb2c3",
            "strix": {
              "id": "vuln-0001",
              "severity": "low",
              "cvss": 3.3,
              "timestamp": "2026-10-25 15:35:52 UTC",
              "target": "cra CLI (local, src/news_watcher.py status_summary / run_is_complete / load_runs)",
              "endpoint": "cra status --runs &lt;folder&gt;",
              "method": "LOCAL",
              "cwe": "CWE-345",
              "impact": "The `status` command is the operator's only safety signal between scheduled `cra run` calls. With write access to the runs folder, a single file hides an earlier REPORT CASE warning and makes status exit 0 with a fresh \"last successful run\" time. The integrity of the status verdict depends entirely on the folder contents. Confidentiality and availability are not affected. Integrity impact is limited to the operator-visible status verdict.",
              "technical_analysis": "`status_summary` takes the latest run as `runs[-1]` after `load_runs` sorts every `*.json` file in the runs folder by its own `finished_at` value. It then decides health from fields the file itself declares: `run_is_complete` checks only `complete is True` and that every entry in `sources` has `status == \"ok\"` (`src/news_watcher.py:585-594`). The other health checks (`report_cases`, `needs_human`, `components.unchecked`, `result.data_gaps`, `sbom_warnings`) are also read straight from the same file. Nothing in the code binds a run record to the process that wrote it. There is no digest, no sequence number, and no cross-check against the genuine run history.\n\n```python title=news_watcher.py startLineNumber=601 endLineNumber=612\ndef load_runs(run_dir):\n    runs = []\n    paths = sorted(Path(run_dir).glob(\"*.json\"))[-MAX_RUN_FILES:]\n    for path in paths:\n        data = _read_json(path)\n        if isinstance(data, dict) and isinstance(data.get(\"finished_at\"), str):\n            runs.append((_parse_time(data[\"finished_at\"]), path.stem, data))\n    ...\n```\n\n```python title=news_watcher.py startLineNumber=658 endLineNumber=659 \n    if run_is_complete(last) and last_time is not None and last_time <= current:\n        lines.append(\"Latest run: all sources ok.\")\n```\n\nThe only protection against a future-dated planted run is `last_time <= current`. A planted file dated slightly before \"now\" passes it. The project records this as an accepted residual risk (PROGRESS.md, \"Cache and run files can be edited by anyone with write access to the project folder\", and PLAN.md section (b)). This report keeps the finding visible because the README warning and status wording are the only mitigation, and status presents the result as a verdict.",
              "remediation_steps": "Treat the status verdict as only as trustworthy as the runs folder, and make that explicit. Two options for the project owner to decide. (a) Add an integrity seal to each run record at save time (a digest of its canonical content, verified in `load_runs`, with unsealed or mismatched files treated as unreadable and surfaced as a warning). This stops accidental and naive edits, not a determined local writer, and needs a decision on key handling. (b) Keep the current design but have `status` print a visible warning when the newest run file is not the newest file written by the tool, and document the limit in the README. Whichever option is chosen, make sure a file that cannot be verified never produces \"Latest run: all sources ok.\".",
              "counterevidence": "1. The project explicitly accepts this. PLAN.md section (b) and PROGRESS.md (\"Cache and run files can be edited by anyone with write access to the project folder ... accepted\") say that fingerprints would only guard against mistakes, and the README is to say so. This report therefore reports a known, accepted limitation, not an unknown bug. 2. The attacker needs local write access to the project folder. The same access already allows editing `evidence/` and the cache, so this is not a privilege gain. The attacker is not the SBOM supplier in the threat model. 3. Future-dated files are rejected (`last_time <= current` and the \"dated in the future\" warning), so the attacker must date the file within the window. This does not stop the attack, but it constrains it. 4. The genuine run record is not deleted, so a human who reads the folder can still see it. 5. The status command still prints the \"26-hour\" and reporting-deadline notes. None of these stop a file that declares healthy fields from being trusted. The check that ran was: does the latest record's own flags say complete, and it does.",
              "confidence": "high",
              "severity_change_conditions": "Raise to Medium if the runs folder can be written by a different principal than the operator (a shared network folder, a scheduler account, or a sync tool), or if an automated consumer (alerting, dashboard, Power BI from PLAN.md phase 5) treats `status` exit 0 as an all-clear. Lower to Informational if the README warning on accepted local write access is confirmed in the shipped docs.",
              "fix_verification": "Informational only. No `fix_after` is provided. The complete fix depends on a product decision that the project has already recorded as an accepted limit (PLAN.md section (b), PROGRESS.md), namely whether to add a seal and how to manage its key, or to add a visible warning. A narrower in-code check (for example, requiring `components` and `result` counts to agree with the flags) would not stop the PoC, because the planted file can declare consistent numbers. Not verified by test run.",
              "poc": {
                "description": "1. In the copy of the project, run a genuine-looking latest run record in a temporary runs folder: `complete` false, `report_cases` 2, `needs_human` true, `finished_at` two hours ago.\n2. Run `python -m src status --runs <folder>`. It reports \"the latest run found 2 REPORT CASE finding(s)\" and exits 1.\n3. Write a second `*.json` file into the same folder with a name in the normal stamp format, `finished_at` one hour ago, `complete` true, `report_cases` 0, `needs_human` false, and healthy `sources`, `components` and `result` values.\n4. Run `python -m src status --runs <folder>` again. The output reads \"Latest run: all sources ok.\" with no REPORT CASE warning, and the exit code is 0.",
                "script_available": true
              }
            }
          }
        },
