          "properties": {
            "security-severity": "3.3",
            "strix_vuln_class_hash": "6ce61c21eb352128181e1a6d99e1d7d81b9db15a3638c166239944b62d482cbc",
            "strix": {
              "id": "vuln-0002",
              "severity": "low",
              "cvss": 3.3,
              "timestamp": "2026-10-25 15:36:07 UTC",
              "target": "cra CLI (local, src/news_watcher.py load_runs / _read_json)",
              "endpoint": "cra status --runs &lt;folder&gt;",
              "method": "LOCAL",
              "cwe": "CWE-770",
              "impact": "Denial of service of the `status` command. A local writer to the runs folder can make `status` consume several gigabytes of memory, slow it down, or crash it with a MemoryError. The operator then cannot check the last run. Data is not disclosed and not modified.",
              "technical_analysis": "`load_runs` selects the newest `MAX_RUN_FILES` (1000) paths by name, and for each one calls `_read_json`, which reads up to `MAX_CACHE_FILE_BYTES` (20 MB) and parses the JSON. Every parsed record that has a string `finished_at` is appended to `runs` and kept until the function returns. `status_summary` then uses only `runs[-1]` and the newest record that passes `run_is_complete`. Per-file size is capped, but the total is not, so memory grows with the file count times the per-file cap.\n\n```python title=news_watcher.py startLineNumber=601 endLineNumber=612 \ndef load_runs(run_dir):\n    runs = []\n    paths = sorted(Path(run_dir).glob(\"*.json\"))[-MAX_RUN_FILES:]\n    for path in paths:\n        data = _read_json(path)\n        if isinstance(data, dict) and isinstance(data.get(\"finished_at\"), str):\n            runs.append((_parse_time(data[\"finished_at\"]), path.stem, data))\n    ...\n```\n\n```python title=news_watcher.py startLineNumber=165 endLineNumber=176 \ndef _read_json(path):\n    try:\n        ...\n        with open(path, \"rb\") as handle:\n            raw = handle.read(MAX_CACHE_FILE_BYTES + 1)\n        ...\n        return json.loads(raw.decode(\"utf-8\"))\n```\n\nA genuine run record is about 13 KB in the demo product. The 20 MB file cap is therefore far above what the tool writes, and the total cap is absent.",
              "remediation_steps": "Bound what `status` loads, not only the size of each file. Keep only the newest run and the newest run that passes the completeness check while reading, instead of retaining every record. Add a total byte budget for the run folder. Make a skipped or oversized file visible: if the budget or the count cap drops a newer file, `status` must print a warning and must not report \"Latest run: all sources ok.\", so that a dropped run cannot hide behind an older healthy run. The project owner should decide the budget size and the wording of that warning.",
              "counterevidence": "1. Write access to the runs folder is required, and the project records that local writers are out of its threat model (PROGRESS.md, accepted). 2. Each file is capped at 20 MB and the count at 1000, so the worst case is bounded, though large. 3. A genuine run record is about 13 KB, so an honest folder never comes close to the limit. 4. The impact is availability of the `status` command only; `run` does not read the runs folder. 5. The attacker is not the SBOM supplier. The check that ran was: does the loader bound the total bytes it reads or keeps? It does not.",
              "confidence": "medium",
              "confidence_rationale": "Memory use per file was measured (821 MB for 40 files). The 1000-file worst case is extrapolated and was not run, so the stated ceiling is unconfirmed. The exact point of failure depends on the host.",
              "severity_change_conditions": "Raise to Medium if an automated job runs `cra status` on a shared or network folder that other principals can write to, so that a remote writer can crash the scheduler's health check. Lower to Informational if the 20 MB per-file cap is reduced to a value that makes the total bounded.",
              "fix_verification": "Informational only. No `fix_after` is provided. A correct fix needs a product decision: the size budget, and whether an over-budget or unreadable newer run must turn the status into a warning. Making the wrong choice would hide the latest run behind an older healthy one, which is the failure this tool exists to prevent. The narrowest code change has not been verified by a test run.",
              "poc": {
                "description": "1. Create a runs folder with 40 files named like `2026-01-01T00-00-NNZ.json`. Each holds `finished_at` and a padding string of about 19 MB, so each file is under the 20 MB cap.\n2. Run `cra status` on that folder, using the copy's `src` package, and measure the peak resident memory and the time.\n3. The result is about 821 MB peak RSS and 2.7 s for 40 files (about 20 MB of RAM per file). Scaling to the 1000-file ceiling is an extrapolation, not a measurement.",
                "script_available": true
              }
            }
          }
