        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-1289",
          "ruleIndex": 2,
          "level": "warning",
          "message": {
            "text": "Slash-separated or upper-case Maven purl is accepted as OK and silently clears an exploited component\n\nA CycloneDX component whose Maven purl uses a slash-separated namespace, such as `pkg:maven/org/apache/logging/log4j/log4j-core@2.14.1`, or an upper-case group or artifact spelling, is accepted as a valid component. OSV does not match these spellings, so `cra run` reports the component as checked with zero findings, a complete result, and exit code 0. The canonical form of the same component is affected by CVE-2021-44228, which is listed in CISA KEV."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "src/sbom_reader.py"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 146,
                  "endLine": 149
                }
              },
              "message": {
                "text": "Reject slash-separated Maven namespaces and fail closed on upper-case spellings"
              }
            },
            {
              "logicalLocations": [
                {
                  "fullyQualifiedName": "cra run --sbom",
                  "kind": "endpoint"
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
          "fixes": [
            {
              "artifactChanges": [
                {
                  "artifactLocation": {
                    "uri": "src/sbom_reader.py"
                  },
                  "replacements": [
                    {
                      "deletedRegion": {
                        "startLine": 146,
                        "endLine": 149
                      },
                      "insertedContent": {
                        "text": "if ecosystem == \"maven\":\n        if \"/\" in parsed.namespace:\n            return unknown(\"maven purl namespace must use dots, not slashes\", ecosystem)\n        if not GROUP_SEGMENT_PATTERN.fullmatch(parsed.namespace) or \"..\" in parsed.namespace:\n            return unknown(\"maven purl namespace has invalid characters\", ecosystem)\n        if parsed.namespace != parsed.namespace.lower() or parsed.name != parsed.name.lower():\n            return unknown(\"maven purl has upper-case letters, which OSV may not match\", ecosystem)"
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ],
              "description": {
                "text": "Reject Maven purl namespaces that contain a slash as non-canonical, since they must use dot-separated group ids. Refuse upper-case Maven group or artifact spellings as UNKNOWN until the tool can confirm the spelling OSV uses, because OSV's Maven matching is case-sensitive. Update the test that currently expects the slash form to be OK so that it expects UNKNOWN, and add regression tests for both forms. This converts the silent clean result for these spellings into UNKNOWN and RESULT INCOMPLETE for human review.",
                "markdown": "Reject Maven purl namespaces that contain a slash as non-canonical, since they must use dot-separated group ids. Refuse upper-case Maven group or artifact spellings as UNKNOWN until the tool can confirm the spelling OSV uses, because OSV's Maven matching is case-sensitive. Update the test that currently expects the slash form to be OK so that it expects UNKNOWN, and add regression tests for both forms. This converts the silent clean result for these spellings into UNKNOWN and RESULT INCOMPLETE for human review."
              }
            }
          ],
          "partialFingerprints": {
            "primaryLocationLineHash": "37cfa1f08c745d97e6022c0d430dfb0b10ce093039f60647645506553b99e9b9"
          },
          "properties": {
            "security-severity": "5.5",
            "strix_vuln_class_hash": "d9cd2fcb8292f0cc2bf5088b9f71a5e8a55505d6b5b6a17dec0117e0dcd64134",
            "strix": {
              "id": "vuln-0004",
              "severity": "medium",
              "cvss": 5.5,
              "timestamp": "2026-10-25 15:42:15 UTC",
              "target": "cra CLI (local): src/sbom_reader.py (_read_component) and src/cli.py (run)",
              "endpoint": "cra run --sbom",
              "method": "LOCAL",
              "cwe": "CWE-1289",
              "impact": "A supplier-supplied or hand-edited SBOM can make the tool report a vulnerable Maven component as clean. The run record is marked complete, the console prints `No exploited vulnerabilities found in the checked components (all sources ok)`, and `cra status` reports `Latest run: all sources ok.` A KEV-listed affected component is therefore never raised for the 24-hour early-warning decision. The same silence applies to every advisory for that component, not only the exploited one.",
              "technical_analysis": "The SBOM reader validates each component purl and then rebuilds a canonical purl from its parts. For Maven, the namespace is split on the slash character and every segment is accepted independently, so org/apache/logging/log4j passes as a namespace (sbom_reader.py lines 146 to 149). The group-field check only compares the purl namespace with its dotted form, so a matching group field also passes. The name and group checks use exact comparison, so upper-case spellings pass too. The canonical purl is then sent to OSV unchanged. OSV querybatch is case-sensitive and does not normalize slashes, so it returns no advisories for these spellings. In matcher.evaluate the component therefore has an empty advisory list, produces no finding, and is counted in checked. match_vulnerability only returns UNKNOWN when OSV listed the vulnerability for that purl, so nothing downstream notices the mismatch. feeds.complete is then true, so the run record and cra status both report success. An existing test, test_double_slash_in_a_namespace_is_cleaned_up_not_kept, pins the acceptance of the slash form as OK. Measured against live OSV on the same component: the canonical purl returns 7 advisories, including GHSA-jfh8-c2jp-5v3q (CVE-2021-44228, KEV-listed). The slash-namespace, upper-case group, and upper-case artifact variants each return 0 advisories. Root cause: the reader accepts non-canonical identifiers as equivalent to the canonical component without checking that they are spelled the way the vulnerability database spells them, and an absent advisory answer is treated as a clean result.",
              "remediation_steps": "Reject Maven purl namespaces that contain a slash as non-canonical, since they must use dot-separated group ids. Refuse upper-case Maven group or artifact spellings as UNKNOWN until the tool can confirm the spelling OSV uses, because OSV's Maven matching is case-sensitive. Update the test that currently expects the slash form to be OK so that it expects UNKNOWN, and add regression tests for both forms. This converts the silent clean result for these spellings into UNKNOWN and RESULT INCOMPLETE for human review.",
              "counterevidence": "Checked: mainstream generators (CycloneDX Maven plugin, Syft) emit dot-separated canonical namespaces, so the trigger needs a hand-written, edited, or defective SBOM. This lowers likelihood but not the validity of the defect. Checked whether check-sbom or run surfaces anything: check-sbom prints OK and run prints only Components checked: 1, so the operator is not warned. Checked whether the matcher's osv_listed safety rule catches it: it does not, because OSV returned nothing to compare. Checked whether evidence files or KEV alone could raise the finding: no, KEV is keyed on CVE ids that come only from OSV records, and the record is never loaded. Checked whether the run is marked incomplete: it is marked complete. No control in the reader, matcher, or CLI prevents the silent clear for these spellings.",
              "confidence": "high",
              "severity_change_conditions": "The tool is a local CLI that reads a supplier-supplied SBOM file, so the attack vector is Local with user interaction required (the operator must run the file). If the SBOM were ingested automatically from a network channel without operator action, the rating would rise to High (7.5); cross-checking component identity against a second source would lower it to Low.",
              "fix_verification": "Gate 1 security closure: in the patched copy the slash-namespace SBOM now makes run exit 1 with RESULT INCOMPLETE, and check-sbom marks the component UNKNOWN with reason maven purl namespace must use dots, not slashes. The source to sink path is blocked at the reader, so the component never reaches the OSV query as a clean component. Gate 2 bypass review: the upper-case group, upper-case artifact, and double-slash variants are also UNKNOWN; qualifiers still canonicalize to the same namespace; the dot-dot check still applies to the namespace. The remaining gap is that a legitimate mixed-case Maven group id now becomes UNKNOWN instead of being checked; this is an intentional fail-closed trade-off stated in the remediation. Gate 3 preserved behavior: the canonical SBOM still exits 3 and prints the two REPORT CASE lines for CVE-2021-44228 and CVE-2021-45046, and the group field match still works for canonical input. Gate 4 how checked: executed, the live CLI run for slash and canonical forms and python3 -m pytest in the working copy (412 passed, 2 skipped, after updating the one test that pinned the slash form as OK and adding regression tests for slash and upper-case spellings). Not executed: a mutation check that removes the new branch to confirm the new regression tests fail.",
              "poc": {
                "description": "1. Create a product config with a product name, a manufacturer, and one EU member state. 2. Create a CycloneDX SBOM with one component whose purl is pkg:maven/org/apache/logging/log4j/log4j-core@2.14.1 (slash-separated namespace). 3. Run check-sbom on the SBOM; the component is listed as OK. 4. Run run with the SBOM, the config, and empty cache, runs and evidence folders; the console reports zero findings, Components checked: 1, and exit code 0, and the saved run record has complete set to true and no findings. 5. Repeat with the canonical purl pkg:maven/org.apache.logging.log4j/log4j-core@2.14.1; the same run reports a REPORT CASE for CVE-2021-44228 and exits with code 3, which shows the component is affected and KEV-listed. 6. Repeat with pkg:maven/ORG.apache.logging.log4j/log4j-core@2.14.1 (upper-case group); the result matches step 4.",
                "script_available": true
              }
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-697",
          "ruleIndex": 3,
          "level": "note",
          "message": {
            "text": "Same-version range boundaries misordered, hiding reintroduced affected versions\n\nThe OSV range walk in the CRA matcher sorts a fixed boundary before a later introduced boundary at the same version. A version that is affected again after the reintroduction is therefore computed as not affected. The evaluation step then reports it as UNKNOWN, so an actively exploited (KEV-listed) affected component gets urgent human review instead of a report case."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "src/matcher.py"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 112,
                  "endLine": 112
                }
              },
              "message": {
                "text": "Sort range events so the written order decides ties at the same version"
              }
            },
            {
              "logicalLocations": [
                {
                  "fullyQualifiedName": "src/matcher.py::_intervals",
                  "kind": "endpoint"
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
          "fixes": [
            {
              "artifactChanges": [
                {
                  "artifactLocation": {
                    "uri": "src/matcher.py"
                  },
                  "replacements": [
                    {
                      "deletedRegion": {
                        "startLine": 112,
                        "endLine": 112
                      },
                      "insertedContent": {
                        "text": "parsed.sort(key=lambda item: item[0])  # same version: keep the order the events were written in"
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ],
              "description": {
                "text": "Keep the order in which range events were written when two events share a version, instead of forcing every start before every end. Then the reintroduction at the same version is applied after the preceding fixed event, and the open span is kept. Add a regression test with a same-version fixed and introduced pair, asserting AFFECTED for a version after the reintroduction and an UNKNOWN or AFFECTED result (never NOT_AFFECTED) for a reversed-order tie.",
                "markdown": "Keep the order in which range events were written when two events share a version, instead of forcing every start before every end. Then the reintroduction at the same version is applied after the preceding fixed event, and the open span is kept. Add a regression test with a same-version fixed and introduced pair, asserting AFFECTED for a version after the reintroduction and an UNKNOWN or AFFECTED result (never NOT_AFFECTED) for a reversed-order tie."
              }
            }
          ],
          "partialFingerprints": {
            "primaryLocationLineHash": "8dd2ae972075e412a168f07771e69d34d57e2cec6bb06c37108b86f10d9a62d4"
          },
          "properties": {
            "security-severity": "3.7",
            "strix_vuln_class_hash": "d45767e85c9424a42eb50300f5845cebe1e54a246954f80a803239648484834a",
            "strix": {
              "id": "vuln-0005",
              "severity": "low",
              "cvss": 3.7,
              "timestamp": "2026-10-25 15:43:28 UTC",
              "target": "cra-scan (src/matcher.py)",
              "endpoint": "src/matcher.py::_intervals",
              "method": "N/A",
              "cwe": "CWE-697",
              "impact": "A component affected by a KEV-listed vulnerability through a same-version reintroduction is routed to URGENT_HUMAN_REVIEW instead of REPORT_CASE. The matcher's direct result for the same version is NOT_AFFECTED. Demonstrated with a crafted OSV-style record; the scope is limited to range shapes with a same-version end and start inside one range.",
              "technical_analysis": "_intervals converts OSV introduced, fixed and last_affected events into start, end and inclusive spans. It sorts the parsed events by (version, is_end), so at an equal version every start is placed before every end. The loop then skips a start while a span is already open. For a range introduced 2.0-beta9, fixed 2.12.2, introduced 2.12.2, the sorted order is introduced 2.0-beta9, introduced 2.12.2, fixed 2.12.2. The second start is skipped as redundant, and the fixed event closes the span at 2.12.2. The span that the event list describes as open from 2.12.2 onward is lost, so the version 2.14.1 falls outside every span and match_vulnerability returns NOT_AFFECTED (maven_numeric_range). Only the written order distinguishes this case from a zero-width span, so ties must keep the order in which the events were written. evaluate passes osv_listed=True, so the matcher's NOT_AFFECTED is converted to UNKNOWN (osv_says_affected_but_our_check_disagrees). For a KEV-listed CVE this yields URGENT_HUMAN_REVIEW rather than REPORT_CASE.",
              "remediation_steps": "Keep the order in which range events were written when two events share a version, instead of forcing every start before every end. Then the reintroduction at the same version is applied after the preceding fixed event, and the open span is kept. Add a regression test with a same-version fixed and introduced pair, asserting AFFECTED for a version after the reintroduction and an UNKNOWN or AFFECTED result (never NOT_AFFECTED) for a reversed-order tie.",
              "counterevidence": "The shape requires one range to contain a same-version end and start. In the saved OSV fixtures (50 ECOSYSTEM ranges) no such pair occurs, so this is not produced by the data observed so far. The defect is also masked by design in the evaluation step: a NOT_AFFECTED from the matcher is never shown as NOT_AFFECTED, so the wrong result appears as UNKNOWN with human review rather than as a silent safe answer, and the action is still urgent when the CVE is exploited. The defect does not affect the other range types or the exact version lists.",
              "confidence": "high",
              "severity_change_conditions": "Evidence that real OSV feeds publish same-version reintroduction pairs inside a single range would raise this to medium. Evidence that the only way to reach it is a crafted or compromised feed record would keep it low.",
              "fix_verification": "Gate 1 (applicability): one line changed at src/matcher.py:112; the file compiles. Gate 2 (security closure): the PoC prints AFFECTED maven_numeric_range for match_vulnerability and AFFECTED maven_numeric_range True REPORT_CASE for evaluation after the change. The differential range check reports 0 false negatives and 0 false positives for PyPI and Maven (15,000 records each); before the change it reported 1,051 and 864 false negatives. Gate 3 (bypass review): _intervals is the only place that sorts range events. Reversed-order ties now return UNKNOWN instead of a wrong NOT_AFFECTED; zero-width introduced X plus fixed X still returns NOT_AFFECTED. Gate 4 (preserved behavior): existing tests for out-of-order events, two ranges in one record, pre-release and unreadable limits pass. Gate 5 (repository checks): python3 -m pytest -q tests gives 408 passed, 2 skipped. Not run: bandit and a formatter.",
              "poc": {
                "description": "1. Run from the repository root with the project's Python environment, which has the packageurl and packaging dependencies installed.\n2. Load an OSV-style record for org.apache.logging.log4j:log4j-core whose single ECOSYSTEM range is introduced 2.0-beta9, fixed 2.12.2, introduced 2.12.2, and whose alias is the KEV-listed CVE-2021-44228.\n3. Check pkg:maven/org.apache.logging.log4j/log4j-core@2.14.1. The reintroduction at 2.12.2 is open-ended, so 2.14.1 is affected; the correct answer is AFFECTED.\n4. Evaluate the same component with a KEV list containing CVE-2021-44228. The correct action is REPORT_CASE.",
                "script_available": true
              }
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-754",
          "ruleIndex": 4,
