          "partialFingerprints": {
            "primaryLocationLineHash": "cf546668e809b0f8192ad19bc85167a89bbaf77bad910addba02b7e66dba8643"
          },
          "properties": {
            "security-severity": "5.9",
            "strix_vuln_class_hash": "da16a0fe8c9c25a9b17157fd779d8ea54514285bd7cefbc08ab37b65b05c01fb",
            "strix": {
              "id": "vuln-0007",
              "severity": "medium",
              "cvss": 5.9,
              "timestamp": "2026-10-25 15:44:02 UTC",
              "target": "cra-scan CLI, KEV download (www.cisa.gov known_exploited_vulnerabilities.json), src/news_watcher.py",
              "endpoint": "cra run (fetch_kev -> validate_kev)",
              "method": "GET",
              "cwe": "CWE-345",
              "impact": "An on-path attacker who can serve a modified CISA KEV response can (a) remove specific known-exploited CVEs so that affected components are reported as not exploited, turning a REPORT CASE (the 24-hour early-warning clock) into a RECORD, while the run record and `cra status` show \"all sources ok\" and `complete: true`; and (b) replay the previous KEV list with an advanced `dateReleased`, so CVEs added in the genuine update are absent from the run for the intercepted run. The tool gives no warning for either case.",
              "technical_analysis": "The KEV download is validated in `validate_kev` and stored by `fetch_kev`. The only protection against a shortened list is a count tolerance:\n\n```python title=src/news_watcher.py startLineNumber=224 endLineNumber=225\n    if saved_max_count and len(entries) < saved_max_count * (1 - MAX_KEV_DROP):\n        return \"KEV count dropped by more than 5% compared with the highest count seen before\"\n```\n\n`MAX_KEV_DROP` is `0.05` (`src/news_watcher.py:46`). The check only compares cardinality, so removing any number of entries up to 5% of the list is accepted, and removing entries while adding the same number of invented CVE ids (which pass the CVE pattern, uniqueness and date checks) is also accepted. The download is then stored as a good copy in `fetch_kev` (`src/news_watcher.py:253-254`, `_write_json_atomic` at the end of `fetch_kev`), `status` is set to `ok`, and `Feeds.sources_ok`/`complete` report the run complete. Nothing in the matcher cross-checks the KEV list against the previous copy; `exploited_status` only tests membership in `kev_ids`.\n\nThe removal in the proof of concept targets the two KEV entries that are the only KEV links for the affected log4j-core 2.14.1 records (CVE-2021-44228 and CVE-2021-45046). Removing both is 0.11% of the list, far below the 5% tolerance. The 5% tolerance is explicitly tested as intended behaviour (`tests/test_news_watcher.py`, \"drop of less than 5 percent is accepted\"), so the design choice itself is the root of the completeness gap: the tool asserts completeness and freshness from structure alone.\n\nReplay variant: `validate_kev` only rejects a `dateReleased` older than the saved copy. An on-path response that carries the previous entries with a newer `dateReleased` passes, is saved, and is reported `ok`. A later uninterrupted run then recovers the new entries, so the effect lasts for the intercepted run(s).\n\nThe first run has no saved copy and no baseline, so any list of 1000 or more entries is accepted as complete on the first download. This is a related residual and is not separately demonstrated here.\n\nGit history is not available in the checked-out source, so no commit attribution is given.",
              "remediation_steps": "Reject any new KEV list that does not contain every CVE identifier in the saved copy, and do not allow a count tolerance below the highest count seen. Treat a list that fails this check as `stale` so the run is marked incomplete and the saved copy is kept. Add an authenticity check on the KEV file (a signature or a pinned content hash) before its content is treated as complete, because a structural check cannot detect a replay of the previous list with a newer date. Before merging, confirm CISA's policy on removing entries, since a legitimate removal would then block later downloads until the saved copy is deleted.",
              "counterevidence": "The strongest case against this finding: (1) the attacker needs a TLS-trusted interception position for a US government host, which is a high bar. The Python default verification is on (`http_fetch`), so a plain network attacker without a trusted certificate is rejected; this is why AC is H. (2) The 5% tolerance is a deliberate design choice, documented in `MAX_KEV_DROP` and enforced by a test, so the tool does not claim to detect every omission. (3) The run is not silent in the narrow sense: `Source KEV: ok` is printed. The counterpoint is that the status line says `ok - downloaded and checked`, which asserts completeness that the code cannot verify, and the run record is marked `complete: true`. (4) The first run has no baseline, and the list shrink check is relative to a previous good copy. The replay variant needs no removal and is not stopped by any control in the repository. I did not find a control that prevents the on-path substitution itself.",
              "confidence": "high",
              "confidence_rationale": "The validation logic was exercised end to end on the real TLS download path (`http_fetch`, `fetch_kev`, `validate_kev`, matcher, run record) with a locally trusted certificate; the interception itself is simulated, which is the attacker-position assumption.",
              "severity_change_conditions": "Demonstrating the interception from an unprivileged network position (for example an open network with a trust anchor an attacker can install) would raise the attack complexity assessment and the rating to High; adding an integrity check on the KEV file (signature or pinned content) would lower it to Low.",
              "fix_verification": "Gate 1, applicability: the patch was applied to a copy of the repository. The diff touches only `src/news_watcher.py` and `tests/test_news_watcher.py`; the location blocks match the original text character for character. The `MAX_KEV_DROP` line (46) is informational here: the fix removes it, so it is not an applicable replacement block.\n\nGate 2, security closure: re-ran the reproducer against the patched copy. The omitted list (two CVEs removed) and the padded list (two CVEs removed, two invented ids added) are both rejected with `KEV no longer lists entries that were in the saved copy`, and the saved copy keeps CVE-2021-44228. End-to-end `cra run` with the same omission now yields `Source KEV: stale`, `run.complete=False`, and the saved copy still lists both CVEs. Closure is verified for removal of entries that were in the saved copy.\n\nGate 2 gap: the replay variant (old list, newer `dateReleased`) is not closed. The patched code accepts it and the new genuine CVE is absent for the intercepted run. Closing it needs content authenticity (signature or pinned hash) or a second source, which is outside a local structural check. This patch does not claim to close it.\n\nGate 3, bypass review: traced the changed branch from `fetch_kev` only; `validate_kev` is also called by `load_kev_cache` with no saved ids, so cached-copy loading is unchanged. Tried the padded omission (rejected) and a clean superset with a higher count (accepted, correct). The count check remains as a second guard against a shrinking watermark.\n\nGate 3 gap: a list that adds new entries and drops none is still accepted; that is correct behaviour, but it means a suppressed genuine update is only detected on a later uninterrupted run.\n\nGate 3 trade-off to confirm with maintainers: if CISA ever legitimately removes an entry from KEV, the new check will mark every later download `stale` until the saved copy is deleted. I could not verify CISA's removal policy offline, so this is stated as an open decision, not as a known fact.\n\nGate 3 residual: the first run (no saved copy) still accepts any list of 1000 or more entries.\n\nGate 4, preserved behaviour: the public functions keep their signatures (`validate_kev` gains an optional trailing argument). The normal path (`genuine list: ok`) and the replay-rejection path (older `dateReleased`) are unchanged.\n\nGate 5, repository checks: `python3 -m pytest -q` in the copy: 411 passed, 2 skipped (baseline before changes: 408 passed, 2 skipped). The test `test_kev_drop_of_less_than_5_percent_is_accepted` was replaced by `test_kev_drop_of_a_single_entry_is_rejected_and_old_copy_kept`, and `test_kev_omission_padded_with_invented_ids_is_rejected` was added. The 5% test message assertion was updated to the new message. The 2 skipped tests are the live network tests that require `CRA_LIVE_TEST=1`.",
              "poc": {
                "description": "1. Start from the repository's saved KEV list (1754 entries, includes CVE-2021-44228 and CVE-2021-45046), and a clean cache folder.\n2. Run `cra run` with a single affected component (log4j-core 2.14.1) and a KEV download that is served over TLS from an on-path position. The genuine download produces a REPORT CASE and exit code 3.\n3. Serve a KEV list that is structurally valid (count equals the number of entries, all cveID and dateAdded fields well formed, unique IDs, newer or equal dateReleased) and omits CVE-2021-44228 and CVE-2021-45046.\n4. Observe that the KEV source is reported `ok - downloaded and checked`, the run record has `complete: true`, the finding is `RECORD` with `exploited: false`, the exit code drops to 1, and the saved KEV copy no longer contains the two CVEs.\n5. Repeat step 3 with the omitted list padded by two invented valid CVE identifiers so that the entry count is unchanged; the same result occurs.",
                "script_available": true
              }
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-345",
          "ruleIndex": 0,
          "level": "note",
          "message": {
            "text": "Future-dated KEV response freezes the saved KEV copy until the operator intervenes\n\n`validate_kev` accepts any well-formed `dateReleased`, including dates far in the future. One intercepted KEV response with a future date replaces the saved good copy. Every later genuine KEV download is then rejected as \"older than the saved copy (possible replay)\", so the tool keeps using the frozen copy and no longer picks up new KEV entries until the operator deletes the cache file."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "src/news_watcher.py"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 198,
                  "endLine": 199
                }
              },
              "message": {
                "text": "Reject a KEV dateReleased in the future"
              }
            },
            {
              "logicalLocations": [
                {
                  "fullyQualifiedName": "cra run (fetch_kev -> validate_kev)",
                  "kind": "endpoint"
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
          "fixes": [
            {
              "artifactChanges": [
                {
                  "artifactLocation": {
                    "uri": "src/news_watcher.py"
                  },
                  "replacements": [
                    {
                      "deletedRegion": {
                        "startLine": 198,
                        "endLine": 199
                      },
                      "insertedContent": {
                        "text": "if released is None:\n        return \"KEV dateReleased is not a valid time\"\n    if released > utc_now() + timedelta(days=1):\n        return \"KEV dateReleased is in the future\""
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ],
              "description": {
                "text": "Reject a KEV `dateReleased` that is later than the current time plus a small clock-skew allowance, in the same validation function that already rejects malformed dates. Keep the rejection in the download path so a rejected response never replaces the saved copy. Also make the saved-copy loader apply the same check so a copy saved before this fix is discarded instead of being loaded.",
                "markdown": "Reject a KEV `dateReleased` that is later than the current time plus a small clock-skew allowance, in the same validation function that already rejects malformed dates. Keep the rejection in the download path so a rejected response never replaces the saved copy. Also make the saved-copy loader apply the same check so a copy saved before this fix is discarded instead of being loaded."
              }
            }
          ],
          "partialFingerprints": {
            "primaryLocationLineHash": "7e1a99cdfcc56a98790515c378fc3012cb39dd6c96b6908a7823c5560c9310ba"
          },
          "properties": {
            "security-severity": "3.7",
            "strix_vuln_class_hash": "6f6f9022b6e5615885a348c1708501379a92ac89615e12d77c0e528792f332d3",
            "strix": {
              "id": "vuln-0008",
              "severity": "low",
              "cvss": 3.7,
              "timestamp": "2026-10-25 15:44:25 UTC",
              "target": "cra-scan CLI, KEV download (www.cisa.gov known_exploited_vulnerabilities.json), src/news_watcher.py",
              "endpoint": "cra run (fetch_kev -> validate_kev)",
              "method": "GET",
              "cwe": "CWE-345",
              "impact": "A single on-path response with a future `dateReleased` makes the KEV source `stale` on every later run, so each run is marked RESULT INCOMPLETE and newly added KEV entries are never recognised. Affected components with no KEV match are then routed to urgent human review rather than checked against the current list. The condition persists until someone deletes the saved KEV file, and the status message blames a \"possible replay\" that did not happen.",
              "technical_analysis": "`validate_kev` checks that `dateReleased` parses as a timezone-aware time, but has no upper bound:\n\n```python title=src/news_watcher.py startLineNumber=198 endLineNumber=199\n    if released is None:\n        return \"KEV dateReleased is not a valid time\"\n```\n\nThe replay check in the same function then compares every later genuine download against this future value:\n\n```python title=src/news_watcher.py startLineNumber=228 endLineNumber=230\n    saved_time = _parse_time(saved_released)\n    if saved_time is not None and released < saved_time:\n        return \"KEV dateReleased is older than the saved copy (possible replay)\"\n```\n\nA future `saved_released` makes every genuine download \"older\", so `fetch_kev` falls back to the saved copy (`status: stale`). `fetch_kev` only overwrites the saved copy on a download that passes `validate_kev`, so the frozen copy is never replaced. `load_kev_cache` also re-validates the saved copy with `validate_kev`, which does not reject a future date, so the poisoned copy keeps being loaded.\n\nThe matcher gates non-exploited verdicts on the KEV status (`kev_ok` in `src/matcher.py`), so the freeze produces urgent review rather than a silent \"not exploited\"; the impact is therefore availability and a persistent incomplete state rather than a silent integrity failure.",
              "remediation_steps": "Reject a KEV `dateReleased` that is later than the current time plus a small clock-skew allowance, in the same validation function that already rejects malformed dates. Keep the rejection in the download path so a rejected response never replaces the saved copy. Also make the saved-copy loader apply the same check so a copy saved before this fix is discarded instead of being loaded.",
              "counterevidence": "(1) The attacker needs an on-path position with a trusted certificate for www.cisa.gov, the same precondition as the other KEV finding. (2) The impact is a persistent but visible state: every run prints `Source KEV: stale` and `RESULT INCOMPLETE`, so the operator is alerted, and the matcher escalates unmatched findings to urgent human review instead of marking them not exploited. That limits the consequence to availability and operator workload, which is why the rating is Low. (3) Deleting the saved KEV file clears the freeze, so recovery is possible. I checked whether a future date could be used to suppress a silent verdict: `src/matcher.py` gates non-exploited findings on `kev_ok`, so no silent \"not exploited\" result was produced in the reproduction.",
              "confidence": "high",
              "confidence_rationale": "Reproduced end to end on the real KEV code path (fetch_kev, validate_kev, saved copy, run record) with the same simulated on-path response as the other KEV report.",
              "severity_change_conditions": "If the freeze could be made silent (for example, if the matcher trusted the frozen copy without the stale status gate), the rating would rise to Medium; a check that the saved copy is not older than the stale threshold would lower the rating to informational.",
              "fix_verification": "Gate 1, applicability: a two-line insertion after the existing `dateReleased` check; `utc_now` and `timedelta` are already defined and imported in `src/news_watcher.py`. The original lines 198-199 match the `fix_before` text.\n\nGate 2, security closure: the future-dated list is rejected with `KEV dateReleased is in the future` and the saved copy is not replaced. The genuine newer list is then accepted and includes the new entry (reproducer steps 2 and 3 after the patch).\n\nGate 3, bypass review: a `dateReleased` up to one day ahead is still accepted, so a freeze of up to one day is possible per intercepted response; the check bounds the freeze rather than removing it. `load_kev_cache` re-runs `validate_kev` on the saved copy, so a copy saved before the patch with a future date is now discarded on load and the next genuine download is accepted, which restores the baseline. Clock skew of more than one day on the operator's machine would reject a genuine list (fail-safe, `stale`).\n\nGate 4, preserved behaviour: the normal path (`dateReleased` at or before now) is unchanged, and the replay check still rejects older dates.\n\nGate 5, repository checks: `python3 -m pytest -q` in the copy: 411 passed, 2 skipped, including the new `test_kev_date_released_in_the_future_is_rejected`. The test asserts that the future-dated list is reported as a problem, which the unmodified `validate_kev` does not do (the reproducer shows `ok` for the same input).",
              "poc": {
                "description": "1. Start with a clean cache folder and run `cra run` against a genuine KEV list (dateReleased 2026-10-20). The KEV status is `ok`.\n2. Serve a KEV list with the same entries and `dateReleased` set to `2099-01-01T00:00:00Z`. The response is accepted: KEV status is `ok` and the saved copy now carries the future date.\n3. Serve a genuine newer list (dateReleased 2026-10-21) that adds one new KEV CVE. The response is rejected with `possible replay`, the KEV status becomes `stale`, the run is `RESULT INCOMPLETE`, and the new CVE is absent from the saved copy on every later run.",
                "script_available": true
              }
            }
          }
        },
