          "properties": {
            "security-severity": "3.7",
            "strix_vuln_class_hash": "1ab1c02b89ad45891b07fab1ca6ce2c62207f31b98bd705591f6cd79bf8490c4",
            "strix": {
              "id": "vuln-0011",
              "severity": "low",
              "cvss": 3.7,
              "timestamp": "2026-10-25 15:45:12 UTC",
              "target": "cra-scan CLI, OSV batch and detail download, src/news_watcher.py",
              "endpoint": "cra run (_parse_batch_result -> fetch_osv -> _vuln_cache_path -> safe_join)",
              "method": "POST",
              "cwe": "CWE-755",
              "impact": "An on-path attacker who can serve one OSV batch response can stop every `cra run` from completing while that response is served. The tool fails safe (the run is marked incomplete and the operator is told), so no false \"nothing found\" result is produced; the impact is an availability loss for the scheduled check.",
              "technical_analysis": "The id is validated by `VULN_ID_PATTERN`, which accepts a trailing dot:\n\n```python title=src/news_watcher.py startLineNumber=56 endLineNumber=56\nVULN_ID_PATTERN = re.compile(r\"(?!.*\\.\\.)[A-Za-z0-9][A-Za-z0-9._-]{0,63}\")  # no \"..\" anywhere\n```\n\nThe id then reaches `_vuln_cache_path`, which builds the file name `osv-<id>.json` and passes it to `safe_join`. `safe_join` rejects any name that ends with a dot (`src/safe_paths.py`, `name.endswith(\".\")`), so the same id is refused with a `ValueError`. The call site in `fetch_osv` is outside every `try` block:\n\n```python title=src/news_watcher.py startLineNumber=511 endLineNumber=511\n        path = _vuln_cache_path(cache_dir, vuln_id)\n```\n\nOnly `FetchError` is caught in that loop, so the `ValueError` propagates to `collect` and `_run`. `_run` catches `Exception`, prints `RESULT INCOMPLETE - internal error (ValueError)` and saves a failed run record. The two validators agree on every other id class; the mismatch is limited to the trailing dot. Identifiers with a trailing dot do not occur in OSV data, which is why this is a robustness defect in a feed-controlled input rather than a data-integrity issue.",
              "remediation_steps": "Make the identifier pattern match exactly what the cache-path check accepts, so that a trailing dot is rejected when the batch answer is parsed. Also catch a rejected identifier at the call site in `fetch_osv` so that a single bad identifier cannot abort the whole run.",
              "counterevidence": "(1) The attacker needs the on-path position described above. (2) The run fails closed: the record is saved as incomplete and the output says so, so no wrong verdict is produced. (3) `safe_join` itself is correct: it rejects the name and no file is written outside the cache. The defect is the inconsistency between the two validators, and the consequence is an availability loss for the run, which is why the rating is Low. I checked that the id cannot reach a path outside the cache folder (the rejection happens before any write).",
              "confidence": "high",
              "confidence_rationale": "Reproduced directly on the unmodified `fetch_osv` and through the `cra run` safety net; the on-path response is simulated.",
              "severity_change_conditions": "If the abort were reachable without an on-path position (for example through a crafted SBOM value that the tool echoes back as an id), the rating would stay Low because the run still fails closed; a path that writes or reads outside the cache would be a separate, higher finding.",
              "fix_verification": "Gate 1, applicability: the regex change keeps the same maximum length (64 characters), keeps the single-character ids, and keeps the `..` lookahead. The original line 56 matches `fix_before`. The character class change only removes the trailing `.` case.\n\nGate 2, security closure: with the patched pattern, `GHSA-abcd-efgh-ijkl.` fails the batch-answer check, the component gets no answer, and the run reports `Source OSV: failed - no answer from OSV and no saved answer for one component` instead of an internal error. The `ValueError` can no longer arise from this id class.\n\nGate 3, bypass review: the other ways to reach `safe_join` with an unsafe name are the `..` lookahead, the slash and backslash exclusion, and the Windows device-name check; each was checked directly in the reproducer, and `safe_join` still rejects names that the pattern would now allow (for example `osv-a-.json` is allowed and is a plain name). Identifiers that differ only by a trailing dot are all rejected by the new pattern. An identifier of 64 characters ending in a letter is accepted, as before.\n\nGate 4, preserved behaviour: legitimate OSV identifiers (CVE-, GHSA-, PYSEC-, and others that do not end with a dot) are accepted unchanged; the test fixtures use only such ids.\n\nGate 5, repository checks: `python3 -m pytest -q` in the copy: 411 passed, 2 skipped, including the new parametrised case `GHSA-abcd-efgh-ijkl.` in `test_osv_invalid_vulnerability_id_is_rejected`.",
              "poc": {
                "description": "1. Serve an OSV batch response whose result for one component lists a vulnerability with id `GHSA-abcd-efgh-ijkl.` (trailing dot), with a valid modified time.\n2. Run `cra run` with an SBOM that contains that component and a valid KEV list.\n3. Observe the internal-error abort, the failed run record, and that the other components are not reported.",
                "script_available": true
              }
            }
          }
