          "ruleId": "CWE-367",
          "ruleIndex": 7,
          "level": "note",
          "message": {
            "text": "Concurrent run records in the same second overwrite each other\n\n`save_run` checks whether the run file name is free and then writes it in a separate step. Two runs that finish in the same UTC second can both see the name as free and both write it, so the later write replaces the earlier run record. In the measured case, 16 concurrent saves left one or two files on disk. A complete run can therefore replace an incomplete one in the same second, and `cra status` then shows the replacement."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "src/news_watcher.py"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 575,
                  "endLine": 581
                }
              },
              "message": {
                "text": "Claim the run file name with O_EXCL before writing"
              }
            },
            {
              "logicalLocations": [
                {
                  "fullyQualifiedName": "cra run (save_run)",
                  "kind": "endpoint"
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
          "fixes": [
            {
              "artifactChanges": [
                {
                  "artifactLocation": {
                    "uri": "src/news_watcher.py"
                  },
                  "replacements": [
                    {
                      "deletedRegion": {
                        "startLine": 575,
                        "endLine": 581
                      },
                      "insertedContent": {
                        "text": "stamp = now().strftime(\"%Y-%m-%dT%H-%M-%SZ\")\n    path = run_dir / f\"{stamp}.json\"\n    counter = 1\n    run_dir.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)\n    while True:\n        try:\n            os.close(os.open(path, os.O_WRONLY | os.O_CREAT | os.O_EXCL, 0o644))\n            break\n        except FileExistsError:\n            counter += 1\n            path = run_dir / f\"{stamp}-{counter:04d}.json\"  # zero-padded so the order stays right\n    try:\n        _write_json_atomic(path, run)\n    except BaseException:\n        path.unlink(missing_ok=True)\n        raise"
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ],
              "description": {
                "text": "Claim the run file name atomically before writing it, so that a second process cannot take the same name, and keep the atomic replace for the content. If the claim fails, move to the next numbered name. Add a regression test that runs concurrent saves in one second rather than only sequential ones.",
                "markdown": "Claim the run file name atomically before writing it, so that a second process cannot take the same name, and keep the atomic replace for the content. If the claim fails, move to the next numbered name. Add a regression test that runs concurrent saves in one second rather than only sequential ones."
              }
            }
          ],
          "partialFingerprints": {
            "primaryLocationLineHash": "76327e124b34ad143d3d07c9b79e45f34a05039a31be6138f1a74400770e0a43"
          },
          "properties": {
            "security-severity": "2.5",
            "strix_vuln_class_hash": "8987e152b9ddd90b131a015523fa03e8ea0dfa2894dab2e3e15d8e03c9909526",
            "strix": {
              "id": "vuln-0012",
              "severity": "low",
              "cvss": 2.5,
              "timestamp": "2026-10-25 15:45:46 UTC",
              "target": "cra-scan CLI, run record folder, src/news_watcher.py (save_run)",
              "endpoint": "cra run (save_run)",
              "method": "N/A",
              "cwe": "CWE-367",
              "impact": "Two `cra run` processes that finish in the same second (for example an overlapping scheduled job and a manual run) can lose one run record. If the surviving record is a complete run that replaced an incomplete one, `cra status` reports a complete latest run and hides the failure. The impact is limited to the operator's own run history; no remote party can trigger it.",
              "technical_analysis": "The name check and the write are separate steps:\n\n```python title=src/news_watcher.py startLineNumber=575 endLineNumber=581\n    stamp = now().strftime(\"%Y-%m-%dT%H-%M-%SZ\")\n    path = run_dir / f\"{stamp}.json\"\n    counter = 1\n    while path.exists():\n        counter += 1\n        path = run_dir / f\"{stamp}-{counter:04d}.json\"  # zero-padded so the order stays right\n    _write_json_atomic(path, run)\n```\n\n`_write_json_atomic` finishes with `os.replace`, which overwrites an existing file. Between `path.exists()` returning False and the replace, another process can create the same name. The write is still atomic, so no partial file is seen, but the earlier record is gone. Run names have one-second resolution, so the window is the time between the check and the replace in two processes that finish in the same second.\n\nMeasured on the unmodified source: 16 threads calling `save_run` behind a barrier with a fixed time left 1, 1 and 2 run files in three repetitions, from 16 saves. With an exclusive create (`O_EXCL`) claiming the name first, the same test kept 16 of 16 files in every trial. The existing test `test_save_run_does_not_overwrite_a_run_from_the_same_second` covers only sequential saves, which is why it did not catch this.",
              "remediation_steps": "Claim the run file name atomically before writing it, so that a second process cannot take the same name, and keep the atomic replace for the content. If the claim fails, move to the next numbered name. Add a regression test that runs concurrent saves in one second rather than only sequential ones.",
              "counterevidence": "(1) No attacker can trigger the race: the run folder and the scheduler are operator-controlled, and the threat model treats them as trusted. (2) The collision needs two runs that finish in the same UTC second, which is uncommon for a scheduled tool; the reproduction forces it with a barrier. (3) The surviving record is still a valid, atomically written file, so there is no corruption and no exposure of data. The effect is limited to losing one record in a rare overlap, which is why the rating is Low and the attack complexity is high. I did not find a control that prevents the overlap.",
              "confidence": "high",
              "confidence_rationale": "Reproduced on the unmodified `save_run` with real threads and the real write path; the trigger is concurrency only, with no attacker involvement.",
              "severity_change_conditions": "A demonstrated way for a remote or low-privilege party to cause two runs to finish together, or a status path that treats a replaced record as the latest complete run in a way an attacker can exploit, would raise the rating; without that, the finding stays Low.",
              "fix_verification": "Gate 1, applicability: the change is limited to the body of `save_run` (the docstring, the run folder argument and the return value are unchanged). `os` is already imported in `src/news_watcher.py`. The original lines 575-581 match `fix_before`.\n\nGate 2, security closure: with the change, the 16 concurrent saves produce 16 separate files in three of three repetitions (see evidence). The name is claimed before the write, so a second process moves to the next counter value instead of replacing the first record.\n\nGate 3, bypass review: the placeholder file is created empty and is then replaced by the atomic write. A reader that loads the folder in between sees an empty file, which `_read_json` returns as unreadable and `load_runs` skips, so the status is not wrongly complete. If the write fails, the placeholder is removed and the error is raised, so no empty record is left behind. The `FileExistsError` path is the only collision path; other `OSError` values propagate as before.\n\nGate 4, preserved behaviour: the file names (`<stamp>.json`, then `<stamp>-0002.json`, ...) are unchanged, so the time order used by `load_runs` is unchanged. The existing sequential test that checks the same-second naming still applies.\n\nGate 5, repository checks: `python3 -m pytest -q` in the copy: 411 passed, 2 skipped, including `test_save_run_does_not_overwrite_a_run_from_the_same_second`. Gap: the concurrent case is verified by the reproducer above, not by a new unit test in the suite.",
              "poc": {
                "description": "1. Use a run folder that is empty.\n2. Start 16 threads that each call `save_run` with the same fixed time, released together with a barrier (two overlapping `cra run` processes that finish in the same second produce the same condition).\n3. Count the run files afterwards. On the unmodified source the count is far below 16.",
                "script_available": true
              }
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-390",
          "ruleIndex": 8,
          "level": "note",
          "message": {
            "text": "Run record above 20 MB is silently skipped by cra status, which then reports the previous clean run as current\n\ncra run saves its full result, including every finding, with no size limit, but cra status silently skips any run file over its 20 MB read cap. An SBOM with many duplicate components can push the saved run past that cap, so cra status reports an older complete run as the latest run with exit code 0, even when the skipped run contains a REPORT CASE."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "src/news_watcher.py"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 606,
                  "endLine": 607
                }
              },
              "message": {
                "text": "Treat an oversized latest run file as a failed, incomplete run instead of skipping it"
              }
            },
            {
              "logicalLocations": [
                {
                  "fullyQualifiedName": "cra status --runs",
                  "kind": "endpoint"
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
          "fixes": [
            {
              "artifactChanges": [
                {
                  "artifactLocation": {
                    "uri": "src/news_watcher.py"
                  },
                  "replacements": [
                    {
                      "deletedRegion": {
                        "startLine": 606,
                        "endLine": 607
                      },
                      "insertedContent": {
                        "text": "data = _read_json(path)\n        if data is None and path.is_file() and path.stat().st_size > MAX_CACHE_FILE_BYTES:\n            finished = datetime.fromtimestamp(path.stat().st_mtime, timezone.utc)\n            data = {\n                \"finished_at\": finished.isoformat(),\n                \"complete\": False,\n                \"sources\": {\"run_file\": {\"status\": \"failed\", \"detail\": \"the saved run is too large to read\"}},\n                \"components\": {},\n            }\n        if isinstance(data, dict) and isinstance(data.get(\"finished_at\"), str):"
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ],
              "description": {
                "text": "Never skip a run file silently. When a run file exists but is too large or unreadable, status must treat it as the latest run and report it as incomplete, using the file modification time as its time. Also keep saved runs within a size the reader accepts, or raise the reader limit to match the writer, so a legitimate run is not dropped.",
                "markdown": "Never skip a run file silently. When a run file exists but is too large or unreadable, status must treat it as the latest run and report it as incomplete, using the file modification time as its time. Also keep saved runs within a size the reader accepts, or raise the reader limit to match the writer, so a legitimate run is not dropped."
              }
            }
          ],
          "partialFingerprints": {
            "primaryLocationLineHash": "9b1184c23ed66f5322194d24abb6bb742d106c023039d19a05e375ad615ab46c"
          },
          "properties": {
            "security-severity": "3.1",
            "strix_vuln_class_hash": "53dec0e9b439c91768c03cab881d3151ad9eff86fe490a43fb2de2d0e344d698",
            "strix": {
              "id": "vuln-0013",
              "severity": "low",
              "cvss": 3.1,
              "timestamp": "2026-10-25 15:46:02 UTC",
              "target": "cra CLI (local): src/news_watcher.py load_runs",
              "endpoint": "cra status --runs",
              "method": "LOCAL",
              "cwe": "CWE-390",
              "impact": "The status command, which is the scheduled health check, can show a false all-clear while the latest run found an affected and actively exploited component. The run command itself still prints the REPORT CASE and exits with code 3, so the loss is limited to the status view and to operators who rely on it. The effect is an incorrect integrity signal for the early-warning check, with no confidentiality or availability impact.",
              "technical_analysis": "save_run writes the full run dictionary, including result.findings with one entry per component and advisory, using json.dumps with indent 2 and no size check (news_watcher.py save_run and _write_json_atomic). load_runs calls _read_json for each file, and _read_json returns None when the file is larger than MAX_CACHE_FILE_BYTES (20 MB), so load_runs drops the file silently because it is not a dict. status_summary then takes the newest remaining file as last and prints Latest run: all sources ok when that older run was complete. The SBOM controls the size: duplicate component entries each produce a full set of findings for the same advisories. A live run with 1000 identical pypi django 1.0.1 components plus one maven log4j-core 2.14.1 component produced 88007 findings and a 50.5 MB run file. A 14 MB run file from a smaller SBOM (25508 findings) is still read, so the cap is reachable from a small SBOM. This is distinct from the already-filed planted-run-file finding, because the file is written by the tool itself and the defect is the silent skip.",
              "remediation_steps": "Never skip a run file silently. When a run file exists but is too large or unreadable, status must treat it as the latest run and report it as incomplete, using the file modification time as its time. Also keep saved runs within a size the reader accepts, or raise the reader limit to match the writer, so a legitimate run is not dropped.",
              "counterevidence": "Checked: the run command itself prints REPORT CASE and exits with code 3, so interactive runs keep the signal. Checked: the scheduled use is documented as relying on status, so the risk applies to that workflow. Checked: vuln-0001 (planted run file with self-declared healthy flags) and vuln-0002 (memory use of load_runs) are different root causes; this finding is a tool-written file skipped silently. Checked: without an earlier complete run, status reports never fully successful, so the effect needs a prior healthy run. The SBOM is needed to push the file over the cap, and the operator still has to act on status. These conditions keep the severity low.",
              "confidence": "medium",
              "confidence_rationale": "The size dependency and the status outcome were reproduced locally with live OSV and KEV data for the findings count. The operational impact depends on how the operator schedules and reads the status command, which was not observed.",
              "severity_change_conditions": "If the scheduled job exposes only the status exit code to an alerting system with no other review, the rating rises to Medium; if the run command output is always reviewed, it drops to informational.",
              "fix_verification": "Gate 1 security closure: in the patched copy an oversized latest run file now becomes a placeholder run with complete false and a run_file source marked failed, so status prints RESULT INCOMPLETE and exits with code 1 instead of showing the older run as all sources ok. Gate 2 bypass review: the placeholder uses the file modification time, so it sorts as the newest file as intended; unreadable but small files keep the existing behaviour, which the existing test test_a_damaged_run_file_is_ignored still checks. The remaining gap is that a run file far above the cap but still written by the tool keeps being read as failed rather than being re-read, which is the intended safe outcome. Gate 3 preserved behavior: the 14 MB run file still produces the REPORT CASE warning; the healthy runs still give all sources ok. Gate 4 how checked: executed, the status command on a 24 MB run file (now RESULT INCOMPLETE, exit 1) and on a 14 MB run file (REPORT CASE warning), plus python3 -m pytest in the working copy (412 passed, 2 skipped, including a new regression test test_an_oversized_latest_run_file_is_never_skipped). Not executed: a run through the full scheduled workflow on a real Task Scheduler host.",
              "poc": {
                "description": "1. Create a runs folder containing one older complete run with all sources ok. 2. Create an SBOM with 1000 identical CycloneDX components for pkg:pypi/django@1.0.1 and one component pkg:maven/org.apache.logging.log4j/log4j-core@2.14.1. 3. Run cra run with that SBOM and the same runs folder; it exits with code 3 and prints REPORT CASE lines. 4. Run cra status on the runs folder; it prints Latest run: all sources ok. with exit code 0 and does not mention the REPORT CASE. 5. Reduce the SBOM so the run file is under 20 MB; the same status command then prints WARNING: the latest run found 2 REPORT CASE finding(s).",
                "script_available": true
              }
            }
          }
        },
